19:["$","p","3",{"className":"text-gray-700 leading-relaxed","children":"The payer returns its decision as an explanation of benefits, or electronically as an 835 remittance advice. It shows, per service line, the billed amount, the allowed amount, the contractual adjustment, what the plan paid, and what the patient owes — along with reason codes explaining any reduction."}]
1a:["$","div","4",{"className":"rounded-xl border border-primary-200 bg-primary-50/50 p-6","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"block text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-wide text-primary-700 mb-2","children":"Worked example"}],["$","h4",null,{"className":"text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-1","children":"Posting a remittance correctly"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-5","children":"Post a line showing billed $340, allowed $186, plan paid $148.80, patient responsibility $37.20."}],["$","ol",null,{"className":"space-y-4","children":[["$","li","0",{"className":"flex gap-4","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"flex-shrink-0 w-7 h-7 rounded-full bg-primary-600 text-white flex items-center justify-center text-sm font-bold","children":1}],["$","div",null,{"className":"flex-1","children":[["$","p",null,{"className":"font-semibold text-gray-900","children":"Post the payment of $148.80 against the claim."}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mt-1","children":"The actual money received. Straightforward, and the part everyone gets right."}]]}]]}],["$","li","1",{"className":"flex gap-4","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"flex-shrink-0 w-7 h-7 rounded-full bg-primary-600 text-white flex items-center justify-center text-sm font-bold","children":2}],["$","div",null,{"className":"flex-1","children":[["$","p",null,{"className":"font-semibold text-gray-900","children":"Post the $154 difference as a contractual adjustment."}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mt-1","children":"Billed minus allowed. This is written off under the payer contract and may never be billed to the patient — posting it as anything else, or leaving it as an open balance, misstates the practice's receivables and risks an improper patient bill."}]]}]]}],["$","li","2",{"className":"flex gap-4","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"flex-shrink-0 w-7 h-7 rounded-full bg-primary-600 text-white flex items-center justify-center text-sm font-bold","children":3}],["$","div",null,{"className":"flex-1","children":[["$","p",null,{"className":"font-semibold text-gray-900","children":"Move the $37.20 to patient responsibility."}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mt-1","children":"The coinsurance or deductible portion becomes the patient's balance and enters the patient billing cycle. Leaving it against insurance means it is never billed to anyone."}]]}]]}],["$","li","3",{"className":"flex gap-4","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"flex-shrink-0 w-7 h-7 rounded-full bg-primary-600 text-white flex items-center justify-center text-sm font-bold","children":4}],["$","div",null,{"className":"flex-1","children":[["$","p",null,{"className":"font-semibold text-gray-900","children":"Read the reason codes even when the line paid as expected."}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mt-1","children":"Reason codes explain reductions and often flag a pattern — a modifier consistently missing, a code repeatedly bundled. Posting mechanically without reading them means you fix individual claims and never the cause producing them."}]]}]]}]]}],["$","p",null,{"className":"mt-5 pt-4 border-t border-primary-200 text-gray-800","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-semibold","children":"Result: "}],"The account balances to zero against insurance, with $37.20 correctly owed by the patient."]}],["$","p",null,{"className":"mt-2 text-gray-900 font-medium","children":[["$","span",null,{"aria-hidden":"true","children":"🔑 "}],"Payment, contractual adjustment, patient responsibility. Read the reason codes — they are where the recurring problems announce themselves."]}]]}]
1b:["$","$L21","5",{"block":{"type":"knowledge-check","question":"Why is a claim error caught by a scrubber far cheaper than the same error caught by the payer?","options":["Scrubbers charge less per claim than payers do","The scrubbed claim is corrected in minutes and still submits on time, while a payer denial costs weeks of turnaround and consumes filing-limit time","Payers charge a penalty for denied claims","Scrubbed claims are paid at a higher rate"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"The error is identical; the cost is entirely in when it is caught. A scrubber rejection is fixed the same day. A payer denial arrives weeks later, requires rework and follow-up, and consumes time against the timely filing limit — occasionally enough that the claim becomes uncollectible.","optionRationales":["Payers do not charge per claim. The cost is staff time and delay.","Correct. The difference is turnaround time and filing-limit exposure.","Payers do not levy penalties for denials.","The allowed amount comes from the contract and does not change based on how cleanly the claim arrived."]}}]
